A||N||3||kornholio||spark plug wire routing||||
Z||000000||kornholio||09-08-1999||08:15 PM||||How are the wires supposed to be routed on the firewall side?  Mine run under the red intake plenum, but I've seen some pictures that have them over the plenum.  Is it hotter over or under the plenum?  I don't want to fry my wires.  Thanks.||||reg||
Z||000001||theogre||09-08-1999||08:58 PM||||as long as you keep the wire off the exhaust far enough it doesn't matter. <P>Could be in some pictures they are running between the plenum and valve cover. This would make them look like they go thru the plennum, but they really don't. I wouldn't run them thru the plenum just because it a pain in the rear, and there is noplace to anchor looms.||||reg||
Z||000002||mshill||09-09-1999||01:18 AM||||Yeah, I am just in the process of replacing mine and the kit that I have does not have the plastic corrugated (sp?) cover that the old ones had.  I couldn't salvage it because it was so brittle it broke when I tried to remove it.  Is any heat shielding required for the rear plug wires if you run them over the intake plenum?<P>||||reg||
Z||000003||theogre||09-09-1999||02:34 PM||||the plastic covering was for abrasion protection. You can buy it in a number of places. It's not required most of the time. You can use Fiberglass sleeving as an alternate, but it gets cruddy looking and holds dirt and moisture.<P>If you can get them loomed up at the hieght of the valve cover they should be fine. The will get them several inches away which is usually plenty for good wires on a good running car. The only time I've ever seen wires burn is if they contact something or get no airflow at all. The minimal air movment in that area is not a problem to good wires.||||reg||
